"Lipschitz-free spaces and Bossard's reduction argument (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00722)\nciteturn1academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Title: Lipschitz-free spaces and Bossard's reduction argument ... Proch\\'azka, we use the reduction argument of Bossard to prove two results: if a separable Banach space is isomorphically universal for the class of Lipschitz-free spaces over the countable complete discrete metric spaces then it is isomorphically universal for the class of separable Banach spaces, and if a complete separable metric space is Lipschitz universal for the same class of metric spaces then it is Lipschitz universal for all separable metric spaces.\nTitle: Lipschitz-free spaces and Bossard's reduction argument\nAuthors: Richard J. Smith\nDate: Sun Aug 31 07:11:52 2025\n\nMotivated by recent work of E. Basset, G. Lancien and A. Proch\\'azka, we use the reduction argument of Bossard to prove two results: if a separable Banach space is isomorphically universal for the class of Lipschitz-free spaces over the countable complete discrete metric spaces then it is isomorphically universal for the class of separable Banach spaces, and if a complete separable metric space is Lipschitz universal for the same class of metric spaces then it is Lipschitz universal for all separable metric spaces.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Lipschitz-free spaces and Bossard's reduction argument (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395212396_Lipschitz-free_spaces_and_Bossard%27s_reduction_argument)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; We also show that there exist countable complete discrete metric spaces whose Lipschitz-free spaces fail the bounded approximation property and are thus not isomorphic to any dual Banach space. ... there exists a bounded linear ﬁnite-rank operator T:X→X such that ∥T x −x∥⩽ε ... In this vein, there exists a uniformly discrete space ... U or c 0(the latter being Lipschitz universal by Aharoni’s theorem, see e.g. ... In the ﬁnal result of this section, we obtain a lower bound on the complexity of the set\nis not easy to ﬁnd families of metric spaces whose corresponding free spaces are pairwise\n\nnon-isomorphic.\n\nDate: January 7, 2026.\n\n2020 Mathematics Subject Classiﬁcation. Primary 46B20; Secondary 03E15.\n\nKey words and phrases. Lipschitz-free space, universality, descriptive set theory, approximation prop-\n\nerty, bounded approximation property.\n\n1\n\n2 R. J. SMITH\n\nThe ﬁrst example of an uncountable family of separable metric spaces whose free spaces\n\nare pairwise non-isomorphic was given in [21]. These metric spaces are all homeomorphic\n\nto the Cantor set 2 ω. Two more uncountable families of this kind are given in [3,4]. In\n\n[3], each metric space in the family is countable and complete, while in [4] each one is\n\ncountable complete and discrete. The free spaces corresponding to the ﬁrst family are\n\nnon-isomorphic because the weak-fragmentability (or weak Szlenk) index of each one is\n\ndistinct (and countable). The same holds in the case of the second family, this time with\n\nrespect to the dentability index (not to be confused with the w∗-dentability index).\n\nset theory could be used to prove more results about free spaces in the future.\n\nI heard about the results in [4] in a talk given by E. Basset at the Banﬀ International\n\nResearch Station workshop on Functional and Metric Analysis and their Interactions, held\n\nat the Institute of Mathematics, University of Granada, Spain, in May 2025. It occurred to\n\nme that it might be possible to use descriptive set theory, speciﬁcally Bossard’s reduction\n\nargument (see [11, p. 31]), to build on their universality results. I would like to thank\n\nE. Basset, G. Lancien and A. Proch´azka for the fascinating exchanges that ensued. In\n\nparticular, Theorem 1.2 was prompted by a question asked by G. Lancien. I would also\n\nlike to thank M. C´uth, G. Godefroy and A. Rueda Zoca for several remarks which helped\n\nme when I was preparing this paper. Finally, I should stress that I have left out most of the\n\nresults in [3,4] from this introduction purely in the interests of brevity, and I recommend\n\nthat the interested reader should consult these papers in full.\n\n2. Notation and preliminaries\n\nWe use standard Banach space notation throughout. The closed unit ball of a Banach\n\nspace X is denoted B X. Given Banach spaces X and Y, we write X →Y and X≡Y when\n\nX is linearly isomorphic to a subspace of Y and X is linearly isometric to Y, respectively.\n\nWe also use these symbols in the context of metric spaces M and N, with “Lipschitz”\n\nsubstituted for “linear”. By [31, Theorems 3.6 and 3.7], if M →N(in the Lipschitz\n\nsense) then F(M)→ F (N) (in the linear sense). We say that the Banach space X is\n\nisomorphically universal for a given class C of Banach spaces if Y →X whenever Y∈ C;\n\nLipschitz universality of metric spaces is deﬁned analogously.\n\n4 R. J. SMITH\n\nFor concepts pertaining to descriptive set theory, we follow [23]. The sets of natural\n\nnumbers, countable ordinals and inﬁnite sequences of natural numbers are denoted ω,ω 1\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nApproximation properties in Lipschitz-free spaces over groups (https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.09785)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; We study Lipschitz-free spaces over compact and uniformly discrete metric spaces enjoying certain high regularity properties - having group structure with left-invariant metric.Using methods of harmonic analysis we show that, given a compact metrizable group $G$ equipped with an arbitrary compatible left-invariant metric $d$, the Lipschitz-free space over $G$, $\\mathcal{F}(G,d)$, satisfies the metric approximation property.\nTitle: Approximation properties in Lipschitz-free spaces over groups\nAuthors: Michal Doucha, Pedro Levit Kaufmann\nDate: Tue May 19 23:02:11 2020\n\nWe study Lipschitz-free spaces over compact and uniformly discrete metric spaces enjoying certain high regularity properties - having group structure with left-invariant metric. Using methods of harmonic analysis we show that, given a compact metrizable group $G$ equipped with an arbitrary compatible left-invariant metric $d$, the Lipschitz-free space over $G$, $\\mathcal{F}(G,d)$, satisfies the metric approximation property. We show also that, given a finitely generated group $G$, with its word metric $d$, from a class of groups admitting a certain special type of combing, which includes all hyperbolic groups and Artin groups of large type, $\\mathcal{F}(G,d)$ has a Schauder basis. Examples and applications are discussed. In particular, for any net $N$ in a real hyperbolic $n$-space $\\mathbb{H}^n$, $\\mathcal{F}(N)$ has a Schauder basis.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiamond graphs and super-reflexivity * (https://www.weizmann.ac.il/math/gideon/sites/math.gideon/files/uploads/diamond.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 18.1 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; Johnson† and Gideon Schechtman‡ ... Our main focus in this paper is to show (in Theorem 1) that the diamond graphs give a metric characterization of super reflexivity; that is, a Banach space \\(X\\) is not super reflexive if and only if the diamond graphs \\(D_n\\) Lipschitz embed into \\(X\\) with distortion ... Key words: Diamond graph, super reflexivity, \\((p,q)\\) summing operator, dimension reduction.\nDiamond graphs and super-reflexivity *\n\nWilliam B. Johnson† and Gideon Schechtman‡\n\n**Abstract**\n\nThe main result is that a Banach space \\(X\\) is not super-reflexive if and only if the diamond graphs \\(D_n\\) Lipschitz embed into \\(X\\) with distortions independent of \\(n\\). One of the consequences of that and previously known results is that dimension reduction a-la Johnson–Lindenstrauss fails in any non super reflexive space with non trivial type.\n\nWe also introduce the concept of Lipschitz \\((p,r)\\)-summing map and prove that every Lipschitz mapping is Lipschitz \\((p,r)\\)-summing for every \\(1 \\le r < p\\).\n\n# 1 Introduction\n\nIn [BC] Brinkman and Charikar proved that the diamond graphs \\(D_n\\), which were known ([GNRS]) to Lipschitz embed into \\(\\ell_1\\) with distortion independent of \\(n\\), can be used to give a lower bound on dimension reduction in \\(\\ell_1\\); precisely, for each \\(n\\) there are subsets \\(A_n\\) of \\(\\ell_1\\) of cardinality \\(\\overline{A_n}=n\\) so that if \\(\\alpha>0\\) and \\(f_n:A_n\\to \\ell_1^{n^\\alpha}\\), then \\(\\mathrm{Lip}(f_n)\\mathrm{Lip}(f_n^{-1})\\ge c\\alpha^{-1}\\) for some universal \\(c>0\\). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDIAMOND GRAPHS AND SUPER-REFLEXIVITY - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/DIAMOND_GRAPHS_AND_SUPER-REFLEXIVITY)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; # DIAMOND GRAPHS AND SUPER-REFLEXIVITY ... Johnson, Gideon SchechtmanPublication date 29 September 2009 ... absolutely summing operator diamond graph dimension reduction Lipschitz embedding super-reflexive Banach space\n# DIAMOND GRAPHS AND SUPER-REFLEXIVITY\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nJump to:navigation, search\n\nDOI10.1142/S1793525309000114MaRDI QIDQ3398976zbMATH OpenFDO\n\nAuthors William B. Johnson, Gideon Schechtman\n\nPublication date 29 September 2009\n\nPublished in Journal of Topology and Analysis (Search for Journal in Brave)\n\nFull work available at URL https://doi.org/10.1142/S1793525309000114\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nabsolutely summing operatordiamond graphdimension reductionLipschitz embeddingsuper-reflexive Banach space\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nDistance in graphs (05C12) Local theory of Banach spaces (46B07) Duality and reflexivity in normed linear and Banach spaces (46B10) Embeddings of discrete metric spaces into Banach spaces; applications in topology and computer science (46B85)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) DIAMOND GRAPHS AND SUPER-REFLEXIVITY (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/241789254_Diamond_graphs_and_super-reflexivity)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; # DIAMOND GRAPHS AND SUPER-REFLEXIVITY ... Gideon Schechtman ... One of the consequences of that and previously known results is that dimension reduction a la Johnson–Lindenstrauss fails in any non-super-reflexive space with nontrivial type.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# DIAMOND GRAPHS AND SUPER-REFLEXIVITY\n\n  * January 2012\n  * Journal of Topology and Analysis 01(02):177-189\n\nDOI:10.1142/S1793525309000114\n\nAuthors:\n\nWilliam B Johnson\n\n  * Texas A&M University\n\nGideon Schechtman\n\n  * Weizmann Institute of Science\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe main result is that a Banach space X is not super-reflexive if and only if the diamond graphs Dn Lipschitz embed into X with distortions independent of n. One of the consequences of that and previously known results is that dimension reduction a la Johnson–Lindenstrauss fails in any non-super-reflexive space with nontrivial type. We also introduce the concept of Lipschitz (p,r)-summing map and prove that every Lipschitz mapping is Lipschitz (p,r)-summing for every 1 ≤ r < p.\n\n\nDiamond graphs and super-reﬂexivity\n\n∗\n\nWilliam B. Johnson\n\n†\n\nand Gideon Schechtman\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLipschitz-free spaces and Schur properties - ScienceDirect (https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.jmaa.2017.04.047)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The main result states that, under some natural conditions, the Lipschitz-free space o